[{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#Contents","page":"Reference","text":"Pages = [\"95-reference.md\"]","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#Index","page":"Reference","text":"Pages = [\"95-reference.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.AbstractImmutableRNG","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractImmutableRNG\n\nAbstract supertype for immutable random-number generator backends.\n\nUse rand_step with an explicit RNG state to generate samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.AbstractImmutableRNG"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.AbstractImmutableRNGState","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractImmutableRNGState\n\nAbstract supertype for explicit immutable RNG states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.AbstractImmutableRNGState"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.CounterState","page":"Reference","text":"CounterState(counter)\n\nImmutable counter-based RNG state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.CounterState"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.Philox4x32","page":"Reference","text":"Philox4x32(key, counter)\n\nA Philox 4x32 RNG with 10 rounds, backed by PhiloxRNG.jl.\n\nSee Salmon et al., Parallel Random Numbers: As Easy as 1, 2, 3.\n\nUse directly with rand_step. Supported sample types are UInt32, Float32, and Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.Philox4x32"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.Splitmix64","page":"Reference","text":"Splitmix64(seed)\n\nA SplitMix64 counter-based RNG.\n\nSee Steele, Lea, and Flood, Fast Splittable Pseudorandom Number Generators.\n\nUse directly with rand_step. Supported sample types are UInt64, Float64, and Int.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.Splitmix64"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.StatefulRNG","page":"Reference","text":"Compatibility wrapper that exposes the legacy Random.rand API on top of the immutable backend.\n\nThe wrapper owns the current state, but the underlying backend remains purely functional.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.StatefulRNG"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Base.rand-Tuple{StatefulRNG}","page":"Reference","text":"Legacy Random.rand compatibility layer.\n\nAll old-style calls are delegated to the immutable backend while the wrapper stores the current state explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.rand"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.next_state","page":"Reference","text":"next_state(state)\n\nReturn the state following state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.next_state"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.next_state-Tuple{CounterState}","page":"Reference","text":"next_state(state::CounterState)\n\nReturn state with its counter incremented by one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.next_state"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.rand_step","page":"Reference","text":"rand_step(rng, state)\nrand_step(rng, state, T)\n\nGenerate a sample from rng using state, returning (sample, next_state).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.rand_step"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.rand_step!","page":"Reference","text":"rand_step!(rng, state, dest)\n\nFill dest with samples from rng, starting at state, and return the resulting state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.rand_step!"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.randn_step","page":"Reference","text":"randn_step(rng, state)\nrandn_step(rng, state, T)\n\nGenerate a normally distributed sample from rng using state, returning (sample, next_state).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.randn_step"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.randn_step!","page":"Reference","text":"randn_step!(rng, state, dest)\n\nFill dest with normally distributed samples from rng using state, and return the state after the final sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.randn_step!"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ImmutableRNGs.stateful-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{R, Any}} where R<:AbstractImmutableRNG","page":"Reference","text":"Create a mutable compatibility wrapper from an immutable RNG and initial state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ImmutableRNGs.stateful"},{"category":"section","location":"#ImmutableRNGs","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"(Image: Stable Documentation) (Image: Development documentation) (Image: Test workflow status) (Image: Coverage) (Image: Docs workflow Status) (Image: BestieTemplate) (Image: code style: runic)\n\nImmutableRNGs is a lightweight package, that provides an explicit-state interface for random-number generation in Julia. Instead of mutating an RNG object, sampling returns both a value and the state required for the next sample.\n\nThis model is well suited to counter-based generators and parallel or GPU workloads, where RNG state can be passed explicitly between computations.","title":"ImmutableRNGs"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"ImmutableRNGs\")\n\nThen load the package:\n\nusing ImmutableRNGs","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"Create an RNG and an initial state, then pass the returned state to the next call:\n\nrng = Splitmix64(0x123456789abcdef0)\nstate = CounterState(0)\n\nx, state = rand_step(rng, state)          # `Float64` by default\ny, state = rand_step(rng, state, UInt64)\nz, state = rand_step(rng, state, Float32)\n\nTo fill an array, use rand_step!. It mutates the destination array and returns the state after the final sample:\n\nvalues = Vector{Float32}(undef, 1_000)\nstate = rand_step!(rng, state, values)\n\nThe generated type is determined by the destination array:\n\ndest = Vector{UInt64}(undef, 1_000)\nstate = rand_step!(rng, state, dest)\n\ndest = Matrix{Int}(undef, 10, 10)\nstate = rand_step!(rng, state, dest)","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Interface","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"Each backend implements rand_step for an explicit state:\n\nrand_step(rng, state)\nrand_step(rng, state, T)\n\nBoth methods return:\n\nsample, next_state\n\nThe mutating array form fills a destination array and returns only the resulting state:\n\nrand_step!(rng, dest, state)\n\nThe RNG object remains immutable; all progressing state is represented by the explicit state value.","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"#Currently-provided-counter-based-RNGs","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"Splitmix64: a SplitMix64 counter-based RNG supporting UInt64, Float64, and Int.\nPhilox4x32: a Philox 4x32-10 counter-based RNG, backed by PhiloxRNG.jl, supporting UInt32, Float32, and Float64.","title":"Currently provided counter-based RNGs"},{"category":"section","location":"#Compatibility-with-Random","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"StatefulRNG wraps an immutable backend together with its current state, allowing use through Julia's conventional Random.rand interface:\n\nstateful_rng = StatefulRNG(rng, CounterState(0))\n\nrand(stateful_rng)                  # `Float64`\nrand(stateful_rng, UInt64)\n\nvalues = Vector{Float32}(undef, 1_000)\nrand!(stateful_rng, values)\n\nThe wrapper is mutable for compatibility, while the underlying backend retains the explicit-state rand_step interface.","title":"Compatibility with Random"},{"category":"section","location":"#Why-another-random-number-package?","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"ImmutableRNGs provides a small, uniform explicit-state interface for immutable random-number generators. The same rand_step and rand_step! API can be used on the CPU and in GPU kernels, where passing RNG state explicitly is often more convenient than mutating an RNG object.\n\nRelated packages include:\n\nRandom123.jl provides Julia implementations of the broader Random123 family of counter-based generators through Julia's conventional mutable Random.AbstractRNG interface.\n\nPhiloxRNG.jl provides efficient pure-function implementations of the Philox4x32-10 generator for CPU and GPU use.\n\nImmutableRNGs complements these packages rather than replacing them. Its Philox4x32 backend is backed by PhiloxRNG.jl, while ImmutableRNGs adds a backend-independent immutable interface and an optional StatefulRNG wrapper for compatibility with Julia's Random API.","title":"Why another random-number package?"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contributing","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"Contributions are welcome, especially additional RNG backends. Please open an issue to discuss an idea or submit a pull request.","title":"Contributing"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgements","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"This work was partly funded by the Center for Advanced Systems Understanding (CASUS) that is financed by Germany’s Federal Ministry of Education and Research (BMBF) and by the Saxon Ministry for Science, Culture and Tourism (SMWK) with tax funds on the basis of the budget approved by the Saxon State Parliament.\n\nWe extend our gratitude for the support received through direct and indirect funding for this project, especially\n\nMichael Bussmann\nTobias Dornheim","title":"Acknowledgements"},{"category":"section","location":"#AI-assistance","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"Parts of the documentation and code were improved with assistance from ChatGPT-5.6 Terra. All generated suggestions were reviewed and adapted by the project authors.","title":"AI assistance"},{"category":"section","location":"#License","page":"ImmutableRNGs","text":"MIT © Uwe Hernandez Acosta","title":"License"}]
